<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="ch-ftc">
    <title>The Fundamental Theorem of Calculus</title>

    <section xml:id="sec-area-function">
        <title>The Area Function</title>

        <p>
            The definite integral can be defined as a limit of Riemann sums,
            but evaluating a definite integral directly from this definition
            can be cumbersome. The Fundamental Theorem of Calculus connects
            definite integrals with antiderivatives and gives us a much more
            efficient way to compute them.
        </p>

        <p>
            Let <m>f</m> be a continuous function, and define
        </p>

        <me>
            A(x)=\int_a^x f(t)\,dt.
        </me>

        <p>
            The function <m>A(x)</m> measures the net accumulation of
            <m>f</m> from the fixed point <m>a</m> to the variable point
            <m>x</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
            To determine how quickly this accumulated area changes, compare
            <m>A(x+h)</m> and <m>A(x)</m>:
        </p>

        <me>
            A(x+h)-A(x)
            =
            \int_x^{x+h} f(t)\,dt.
        </me>

        <p>
            If <m>h</m> is small, this additional area is approximately the
            area of a rectangle with width <m>h</m> and height <m>f(x)</m>.
            Thus,
        </p>

        <me>
            A(x+h)-A(x)\approx h f(x).
        </me>

        <p>
            Dividing by <m>h</m> gives
        </p>

        <me>
            \frac{A(x+h)-A(x)}{h}\approx f(x).
        </me>

        <p>
            Taking the limit as <m>h\to 0</m> leads to the first part of the
            Fundamental Theorem of Calculus.
        </p>

    </section>

    <section xml:id="sec-ftc-part-one">
        <title>The Fundamental Theorem of Calculus, Part I</title>

        <theorem xml:id="thm-ftc-one">
            <title>Fundamental Theorem of Calculus, Part I</title>

            <statement>
                <p>
                    If <m>f</m> is continuous on an interval containing
                    <m>a</m> and <m>x</m>, and
                </p>

                <me>
                    A(x)=\int_a^x f(t)\,dt,
                </me>

                <p>
                    then
                </p>

                <me>
                    A'(x)=f(x).
                </me>
            </statement>
        </theorem>

        <p>
            In words, differentiating an accumulated integral returns the
            original function.
        </p>

        <example xml:id="ex-ftc-direct">
            <title>Differentiating an integral</title>

            <statement>
                <p>
                    Let
                </p>

                <me>
                    y=\int_0^x \sqrt{1+t^2}\,dt.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Find <m>dy/dx</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    The integral has the form
                </p>

                <me>
                    \int_a^x f(t)\,dt
                </me>

                <p>
                    with <m>f(t)=\sqrt{1+t^2}</m>. Therefore, by the
                    Fundamental Theorem of Calculus,
                </p>

                <me>
                    \frac{dy}{dx}=\sqrt{1+x^2}.
                </me>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="ex-ftc-direct-two">
            <title>Another application of FTC Part I</title>

            <statement>
                <p>
                    Differentiate
                </p>

                <me>
                    F(x)=\int_0^x \left(\sin t-e^{-t}\right)\,dt.
                </me>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    By the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus,
                </p>

                <me>
                    F'(x)=\sin x-e^{-x}.
                </me>
            </solution>
        </example>

    </section>

    <section xml:id="sec-leibniz-rule">
        <title>Variable Limits and the Leibniz Rule</title>

        <p>
            The Fundamental Theorem of Calculus can be combined with the
            chain rule when a limit of integration is itself a function of
            <m>x</m>.
        </p>

        <example xml:id="ex-variable-upper-limit">
            <title>A variable upper limit</title>

            <statement>
                <p>
                    Differentiate
                </p>

                <me>
                    F(x)=\int_0^{x^2}\sin t\,dt.
                </me>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    Let
                </p>

                <me>
                    u=x^2.
                </me>

                <p>
                    The Fundamental Theorem of Calculus gives
                </p>

                <me>
                    \frac{d}{du}\left(\int_0^u \sin t\,dt\right)=\sin u.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Using the chain rule,
                </p>

                <me>
                    F'(x)=\sin(x^2)\cdot 2x.
                </me>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="ex-variable-upper-limit-two">
            <title>Another variable upper limit</title>

            <statement>
                <p>
                    Differentiate
                </p>

                <me>
                    F(x)=\int_0^{\sin x} e^t\,dt.
                </me>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    The upper limit is <m>\sin x</m>. Applying FTC Part I
                    together with the chain rule gives
                </p>

                <me>
                    F'(x)=e^{\sin x}\cos x.
                </me>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <p>
            If the lower limit depends on <m>x</m>, reversing the limits
            introduces a minus sign.
        </p>

        <example xml:id="ex-variable-lower-limit">
            <title>A variable lower limit</title>

            <statement>
                <p>
                    Differentiate
                </p>

                <me>
                    F(x)=\int_{x^2}^{5} t^2\,dt.
                </me>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    Reverse the limits:
                </p>

                <me>
                    F(x)=-\int_5^{x^2} t^2\,dt.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Then use FTC Part I and the chain rule:
                </p>

                <me>
                    F'(x)
                    =
                    -\left((x^2)^2\right)(2x)
                    =
                    -2x^5.
                </me>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <theorem xml:id="thm-leibniz-variable-limits">
            <title>Leibniz Rule for Variable Limits</title>

            <statement>
                <p>
                    If
                </p>

                <me>
                    F(x)=\int_{u(x)}^{v(x)} f(t)\,dt,
                </me>

                <p>
                    then, under suitable continuity and differentiability
                    assumptions,
                </p>

                <me>
                    F'(x)
                    =
                    f(v(x))v'(x)
                    -
                    f(u(x))u'(x).
                </me>
            </statement>
        </theorem>

    </section>

    <section xml:id="sec-ftc-part-two">
        <title>The Fundamental Theorem of Calculus, Part II</title>

        <p>
            FTC Part I tells us that the accumulation function
        </p>

        <me>
            A(x)=\int_a^x f(t)\,dt
        </me>

        <p>
            is an antiderivative of <m>f</m>. Therefore, any other
            antiderivative <m>F</m> of <m>f</m> differs from <m>A</m>
            by a constant:
        </p>

        <me>
            F(x)=A(x)+C.
        </me>

        <p>
            Evaluating at <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> gives
        </p>

        <me>
            F(b)-F(a)
            =
            A(b)-A(a).
        </me>

        <p>
            Since
        </p>

        <me>
            A(b)=\int_a^b f(t)\,dt
        </me>

        <p>
            and
        </p>

        <me>
            A(a)=\int_a^a f(t)\,dt=0,
        </me>

        <p>
            we obtain FTC Part II.
        </p>

        <theorem xml:id="thm-ftc-two">
            <title>Fundamental Theorem of Calculus, Part II</title>

            <statement>
                <p>
                    If <m>f</m> is continuous on <m>[a,b]</m> and
                    <m>F</m> is any antiderivative of <m>f</m>, then
                </p>

                <me>
                    \int_a^b f(x)\,dx
                    =
                    F(b)-F(a).
                </me>
            </statement>
        </theorem>

        <remark xml:id="rem-ftc-antiderivative-choice">
            <title>Choice of antiderivative</title>

            <p>
                It does not matter which antiderivative of <m>f</m> is used.
                Any constant of integration cancels when computing
                <m>F(b)-F(a)</m>.
            </p>
        </remark>

        <example xml:id="ex-ftc-evaluation">
            <title>Evaluating a definite integral</title>

            <statement>
                <p>
                    Evaluate
                </p>

                <me>
                    \int_1^4 (x+2)\,dx.
                </me>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    An antiderivative of <m>x+2</m> is
                </p>

                <me>
                    F(x)=\frac{1}{2}x^2+2x.
                </me>

                <p>
                    By FTC Part II,
                </p>

                <me>
                    \int_1^4 (x+2)\,dx
                    =
                    \left[\frac{1}{2}x^2+2x\right]_1^4.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Therefore,
                </p>

                <me>
                    \int_1^4 (x+2)\,dx
                    =
                    \left(8+8\right)
                    -
                    \left(\frac{1}{2}+2\right)
                    =
                    \frac{27}{2}.
                </me>
            </solution>
        </example>

    </section>

    <section xml:id="sec-evaluation-notation">
        <title>Evaluation Notation</title>

        <p>
            The expression <m>F(b)-F(a)</m> occurs frequently, so we use the
            shorthand notation
        </p>

        <me>
            \left.F(x)\right|_a^b
            =
            [F(x)]_a^b
            =
            F(b)-F(a).
        </me>

        <example xml:id="ex-evaluation-notation-polynomial">
            <title>Using evaluation notation</title>

            <statement>
                <p>
                    Evaluate
                </p>

                <me>
                    \int_1^2 x^4\,dx.
                </me>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    An antiderivative of <m>x^4</m> is
                    <m>\frac{1}{5}x^5</m>. Therefore,
                </p>

                <me>
                    \int_1^2 x^4\,dx
                    =
                    \left[\frac{1}{5}x^5\right]_1^2
                    =
                    \frac{1}{5}(32-1)
                    =
                    \frac{31}{5}.
                </me>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="ex-evaluation-notation-trig">
            <title>A trigonometric example</title>

            <statement>
                <p>
                    Evaluate
                </p>

                <me>
                    \int_{-\pi/2}^{\pi/2} \cos x\,dx.
                </me>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    Since an antiderivative of <m>\cos x</m> is
                    <m>\sin x</m>,
                </p>

                <me>
                    \int_{-\pi/2}^{\pi/2} \cos x\,dx
                    =
                    [\sin x]_{-\pi/2}^{\pi/2}
                    =
                    1-(-1)
                    =
                    2.
                </me>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="ex-evaluation-notation-odd">
            <title>An odd-function example</title>

            <statement>
                <p>
                    Evaluate
                </p>

                <me>
                    \int_{-2}^{2} x^3\,dx.
                </me>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    Using FTC Part II,
                </p>

                <me>
                    \int_{-2}^{2}x^3\,dx
                    =
                    \left[\frac{1}{4}x^4\right]_{-2}^{2}
                    =
                    \frac{1}{4}(16-16)
                    =
                    0.
                </me>

                <p>
                    This is also consistent with the symmetry property for
                    odd functions on symmetric intervals.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </example>

    </section>

    <section xml:id="sec-indefinite-integral">
        <title>The Indefinite Integral</title>

        <p>
            The symbol for a definite integral includes limits of integration
            and represents a single number. The indefinite integral has no
            limits and represents a family of antiderivatives.
        </p>

        <definition xml:id="def-indefinite-integral">
            <title>Indefinite Integral</title>

            <statement>
                <p>
                    If <m>F'(x)=f(x)</m>, then
                </p>

                <me>
                    \int f(x)\,dx
                    =
                    F(x)+C,
                </me>

                <p>
                    where <m>C</m> is an arbitrary constant.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </definition>

        <example xml:id="ex-indefinite-integral-power">
            <title>An indefinite integral</title>

            <statement>
                <p>
                    Evaluate
                </p>

                <me>
                    \int 2\sqrt{x^5}\,dx.
                </me>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    Rewrite the integrand using exponents:
                </p>

                <me>
                    2\sqrt{x^5}
                    =
                    2x^{5/2}.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Therefore,
                </p>

                <me>
                    \int 2x^{5/2}\,dx
                    =
                    2\left(\frac{x^{7/2}}{7/2}\right)+C
                    =
                    \frac{4}{7}x^{7/2}+C.
                </me>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <remark xml:id="rem-definite-indefinite">
            <title>Definite versus indefinite integrals</title>

            <p>
                A definite integral
                <m>\int_a^b f(x)\,dx</m> gives a single numerical value.
                An indefinite integral
                <m>\int f(x)\,dx</m> gives a family of functions.
            </p>
        </remark>

    </section>

    <section xml:id="sec-net-absolute-area">
        <title>Net Area and Absolute Area</title>

        <p>
            A definite integral gives the net, or signed, area between the
            graph of a function and the horizontal axis. Regions where
            <m>f(x)&gt;0</m> contribute positively, while regions where
            <m>f(x)&lt;0</m> contribute negatively.
        </p>

        <p>
            If we want the total geometric area between the graph and the
            horizontal axis, negative contributions must also be counted
            positively. This is accomplished using the absolute value:
        </p>

        <me>
            \text{absolute area}
            =
            \int_a^b |f(x)|\,dx.
        </me>

        <p>
            To evaluate such an integral, split the interval at points where
            <m>f</m> changes sign.
        </p>

        <example xml:id="ex-absolute-area-sine">
            <title>Absolute area under a sine curve</title>

            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the total area between
                    <m>y=\sin x</m> and the horizontal axis from
                    <m>x=0</m> to <m>x=2\pi</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    The function <m>\sin x</m> is positive on
                    <m>[0,\pi]</m> and negative on
                    <m>[\pi,2\pi]</m>. Therefore,
                </p>

                <me>
                    \int_0^{2\pi}|\sin x|\,dx
                    =
                    \int_0^\pi \sin x\,dx
                    +
                    \int_\pi^{2\pi}(-\sin x)\,dx.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Evaluating each integral,
                </p>

                <me>
                    \int_0^\pi \sin x\,dx
                    =
                    [-\cos x]_0^\pi
                    =
                    2,
                </me>

                <p>
                    and
                </p>

                <me>
                    \int_\pi^{2\pi}(-\sin x)\,dx
                    =
                    [\cos x]_\pi^{2\pi}
                    =
                    2.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Hence the total area is
                </p>

                <me>
                    2+2=4.
                </me>
            </solution>
        </example>

    </section>

</chapter>